--- body ---
## Context

Phase 2 of the close/archive redesign (phase 1 = ticket 0248). The 4-digit ID
range (0001-9999, `src/go/nextid.go`) is a hard cap: `parseIDFromFilename`
ignores IDs >= 10000 and `errRangeExhausted` fails closed at 9999. For a
single-store, single-user backlog this is far outside the design envelope
(git-erg is at ~250 over its whole life, ~40x margin), so the cap is best read
as a "split the store" tripwire, not a routine limit. This ticket adds an
OPTIONAL reclamation path for a store that genuinely accumulates more than 9999
tickets over its lifetime: freeze closed generations into epochs so the live
0001-9999 space recycles.

**Durable identity is the load-bearing constraint.** A LIVE ticket's ID never
changes — renumbering would break branch refs, `Blocked-by:`, and `git blame`,
the property that justifies numeric IDs over content hashes. Reclamation is
achieved by freezing CLOSED generations only, never by renumbering open work.

## Model

Three tiers, by path:

- `tickets/*.erg` — open, current epoch. ID space active.
- `tickets/closed/*.erg` — closed, current epoch. IDs still reserved (next-id scans).
- `tickets/closed/epoch-N/*.erg` — frozen generation. IDs released (next-id skips).

`erg rollover` (NEW verb, additive — `archive` is left untouched) sweeps
`closed/*.erg` into `closed/epoch-<N>/`, where N = max existing epoch + 1. After
a rollover the top-level `closed/` is empty again and the live 0001-9999 space
is free to recycle.

**Epoch directory naming: growing width, not fixed.** `epoch-1` ... `epoch-9`,
then re-pad to `epoch-01` ... `epoch-10` at ten, `epoch-001` at one hundred, and
so on. This makes the epoch counter itself UNBOUNDED — an odometer that grows a
digit rather than a fixed-width counter with its own ceiling — while a plain
`ls | sort` stays correct across the boundary. The re-pad is astronomically rare
(reaching `epoch-10` needs ~90k tickets of history) and touches ONLY the frozen
layer, so live-ticket identity is never affected. It runs as a mechanical
`erg migrate`-style sweep that renames the dirs AND rewrites epoch-qualified refs
in one atomic pass (refs are greppable).

**Reclamation policy (next-id):**

- (1, recommended — ship this first) Gate `rollover` on an EMPTY queue (0 open
  tickets). Nothing is carried across the boundary, `next-id` stays `max+1`, and
  the space resets fully to 0001. Fits git-erg's drain-to-zero cadence — the
  natural rollover moment is "the queue is empty, close the chapter".
- (2, reserve — only if a real need for rollover-with-open-tickets appears)
  Switch `next-id` to MIN-FREE within the current epoch, so a rollover with open
  tickets still in flight reclaims the freed low IDs without renumbering them,
  at the cost of strict chronological-by-ID order. Do not build until needed.

## Relevant files

- `src/go/nextid.go` — `maxIDInDir` uses a recursive `filepath.WalkDir`, so
  everything under `closed/` counts as reserved today. Phase 2 narrows the walk
  to SKIP `closed/epoch-*/` (frozen) while still scanning loose `closed/*.erg`
  (current epoch). `parseIDFromFilename` / `errRangeExhausted` stay as-is — the
  range is still 0001-9999 PER EPOCH.
- `src/go/archive.go` — reuse the shared move primitive 0248 factors out
  (`moveTicketToClosed` or equivalent: MkdirAll + collision-check + Rename) for
  the rollover sweep. `archive` itself is not modified.
- NEW `src/go/rollover.go` — `cmdRollover`: empty-queue gate (policy 1); compute
  next epoch N (max epoch dir + 1, growing width); on crossing a power of ten,
  re-pad existing epoch dirs migrate-style and rewrite epoch-qualified refs;
  `MkdirAll closed/epoch-<N>/`; move every `closed/*.erg` in via the shared helper.
- `src/go/erg.go` — `pathIsClosed` already matches a `closed` segment at ANY
  depth, so `closed/epoch-N/` reads closed for free. Leave untouched; keep the
  read criterion disjunctive (Closed: header OR path).
- ref resolution — a `local-ref` NNNN resolves to `tickets/0042.erg` today. An
  epoch-qualified ref to a frozen ticket (`closed/epoch-1/0042`) needs a NEW
  resolution rule: today's `path-ref` grammar resolves `module/NNNN` to
  `module/tickets/NNNN.erg` from the repo root, so it would miss the archive
  subdir. Spec the qualified-frozen-ref form and wire its resolution where
  `Blocked-by:` / `Superseded-by:` are resolved.
- `tickets/erg` — rebuild the bootstrap binary (`make update-bootstrap-binary`,
  verify with `make verify`).
- `erg spec` §4 (ID assignment / range) and §5 — document epochs, the
  growing-width naming, and the `rollover` verb.
- `src/go/helptext.go` / commands registry — register `rollover`.

## Actions

1. Add `erg rollover` (`rollover.go`), reusing 0248's shared move helper.
   Empty-queue gate (policy 1). Next epoch = max(existing epoch dirs) + 1; on a
   power-of-ten crossing, re-pad existing dirs and rewrite epoch-qualified refs.
2. Narrow `nextid.go`'s walk to skip `closed/epoch-*/`; keep loose `closed/*.erg`
   counted as reserved.
3. Spec the epoch-qualified frozen-ref form + resolution rule; implement
   resolution alongside the existing local-/path-/forge-ref resolvers.
4. Register the verb + help text; update spec §4/§5.
5. Rebuild bootstrap binary; `make verify`; `make check`.

## Test

- `erg rollover` on an empty queue moves all `closed/*.erg` into
  `closed/epoch-1/`, leaves `tickets/` untouched, and `next-id` afterwards
  returns 0001 (space reclaimed).
- `next-id` skips `closed/epoch-*/` but still counts loose `closed/*.erg` as
  reserved.
- Growing width: crossing `epoch-9` -> `epoch-10` re-pads `epoch-1..9` to
  `epoch-01..09` AND rewrites an epoch-qualified `Blocked-by:` accordingly.
- Live tickets are never renumbered by a rollover (assert open-ticket IDs and
  filenames unchanged).
- `archive` and the pre-push nag hook behave exactly as before (additive check).

## Invariants

- Live ticket IDs never change. Rollover touches only the frozen (closed) layer.
- `archive` semantics and the `erg install --push-hook` nag are unchanged.
- Per-epoch ID range stays 0001-9999 (`parseIDFromFilename` / `errRangeExhausted`
  untouched).
- `pathIsClosed` untouched — epoch dirs read closed for free; criterion stays
  disjunctive.
- Re-pad is the ONLY operation that renames frozen tickets; it runs migrate-style
  (dirs + refs in one atomic pass) and only at power-of-ten epoch-count boundaries.

## Exit criteria

`erg rollover` freezes the current closed generation into `closed/epoch-<N>/`
(growing-width naming), reclaiming the live 0001-9999 space; `archive` and the
hook unchanged; `next-id` skips frozen epochs; live tickets never renumbered;
epoch-qualified frozen refs are speced and resolvable; spec §4/§5 + help updated;
bootstrap rebuilt; `make check` and `make verify` pass.
